## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its platform cryptographic libraries (Security.framework / CryptoKit). .NET on macOS relies on the platform crypto provider, so `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS — the managed runtime has no built-in fallback implementation for DSA.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Use OpenSSL on macOS** (if you must keep verifying DSA signatures):

```bash
brew install openssl
```

Then use the OpenSSL-backed type directly:

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

// Load OpenSSL explicitly on macOS
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = new DSAOpenSsl(); // requires OpenSSL available
    dsa.ImportSubjectPublicKeyInfo(publicKeyBytes, out _);
    bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
}
```

You'll need OpenSSL discoverable at runtime (set `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` or install to a standard path).

**Option B — Use BouncyCastle (fully managed, no platform dependency):**

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var pubKey = PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes) as DsaPublicKeyParameters;
var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
verifier.Init(false, pubKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

**Option C — Migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519** (recommended long-term):

DSA is considered legacy. If you control both signing and verification, migrate to `ECDsa` or (in .NET 11+) `Ed25519`, which are supported on all platforms.

---

**Summary:** Apple dropped DSA → .NET can't use it on macOS. Short-term, use OpenSSL or BouncyCastle for verification. Long-term, migrate to a modern signature algorithm.
